# Dependencies
node_modules/
.pnp/
.pnp.js

# Build output
/dist/
/lib/
.vite-cache/
/build/
*.tsbuildinfo

# Local state. Nothing here is recoverable from the repo — see SECURITY.md.
.data/
.wallet-state/
.midnight/

# The stable test wallet's seed. A test fixture, nobody's money — but a
# seed is a seed, and no seed is ever committed.
test-wallet.seed

# Secrets. `.env.example` is committed; `.env` never is.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
seed*.txt
*.mnemonic

# Editors and operating systems
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
.idea/
.vscode/
*.swp

# Test and tooling output
/coverage/
logs/
*.log
npm-debug.log*
.eslintcache

# Superseded files kept out of the way rather than deleted. Not history.
_to_delete/

# THE SHIPPING-LANGUAGE BASELINE. Counts only, one line per file, and it names
# no matched text: a file listing the words it forbids is a file that contains
# them. It is a measurement of this working tree at a moment, regenerated by
# lowering, and it means nothing to a reader of the published repository — a
# clone starts every file it does not know at zero, which is stricter than any
# number written here. It is on no take list either.
.maintainer/language/check-shipping-language.baseline

# The convenience scripts and everything they print. Local tooling, not the
# project. Their reports live in logs/, which is ignored above.
*.command

# Vendored proving-key material (`npm run vendor-keys`). The artefacts are big
# (~116 MB if every parameter size is held) and re-downloadable — they are
# verified against the committed pins on every use. The manifest of SHA-256
# pins IS committed: it is the integrity anchor that makes re-downloading safe.
src/app/public/keys/*
!src/app/public/keys/manifest.json

# Local vendoring tooling, same rule as the other scripts above.
#
# WRITTEN AS A PATH, NOT A BARE NAME. As `vendor-keys.mjs` this
# rule matched at ANY depth, so its scope was invisible: two high-entropy
# sweeps of this same tree disagreed, 40 files against 41, and the difference
# was exactly this file being ignored by one of them and not the other.
#
# THE TEST THIS RULE WAS JUDGED BY, AND IT IS NOT "BARE NAME BAD": anchoring a
# rule NARROWS it, so the question is whether the rule protects something, and
# an unanchored rule is the SAFER error wherever it does.
#
# 34 rules were examined and this is the only one changed. The unanchored ones
# left alone fall in three groups:
#   - SECRET patterns, where matching at any depth is the safe direction:
#     `test-wallet.seed`, `.env`, `.env.*`, `*.pem`, `*.key`, `seed*.txt`,
#     `*.mnemonic`.
#   - Editor, OS and tool litter that genuinely appears anywhere:
#     `.DS_Store`, `Thumbs.db`, `.eslintcache`, `.pnp.js`, `*.swp`, `*.log`,
#     `npm-debug.log*`, `*.tsbuildinfo`.
#   - `*.command`, which is the interesting one and is deliberately NOT
#     anchored: the `.command` files sit at the root today, but the rule's job
#     is to keep local double-click tooling out of the repository wherever
#     somebody puts it next, and being too broad costs nothing here.
# This rule is in none of those groups. It is a TOOL at one known path, it
# protects nothing, and its unanchored form already made two sweeps of this
# tree disagree — so it is the one that had to be anchored.
#
# AND THE RULE IS NOW GONE ENTIRELY, 6 Sep: `scripts/vendor-keys.mjs` SHIPS.
# It is the only thing that verifies the ~117 MB of proving-key material
# against the SHA-256 pins in `src/app/public/keys/manifest.json`, which this
# repository does commit — so excluding the verifier would ship a pin nothing
# checks. The anchoring lesson above is kept because it is about the other 33
# rules, not about this one.

# ── THE SAME LESSON, APPLIED TO THE RULES ABOVE. IT COST A BUILD. ────────────
#
# `lib/` WAS A BARE DIRECTORY RULE AND GIT MATCHES THOSE AT ANY DEPTH, so it
# matched `src/app/lib/` and swallowed `src/app/lib/utils.ts`, which the
# publication allowlist names. Nothing caught it because the allowlist and this
# file are DIFFERENT LISTS and nothing read one against the other: the
# classification step uses its own ignore list, in which `lib` is root-anchored
# and matches nothing here, so it reported a clean partition while staging the
# named file would have failed. Past that failure, five files import
# `src/app/lib/utils.ts` directly and the whole component kit imports it through
# `src/app/kit/cn.ts`, so the wallet does not build.
#
# THE FOUR ANCHORED HERE ARE BUILD AND TOOL OUTPUT: `/dist/`, `/lib/`,
# `/build/`, `/coverage/`. Each names a regenerable artefact at THIS directory,
# each protects nothing, and anchoring them costs nothing.
#
# `logs/` AND `_to_delete/` ARE DELIBERATELY LEFT UNANCHORED, AND THE TEST
# ABOVE IS WHY. Both PROTECT something rather than merely tidying: `logs/` is
# where every home-directory path in this folder lives, and `_to_delete/` holds
# files removed from the product on purpose. Where a rule protects something,
# matching at any depth is the safer error, so those two keep the broad form.
#
# WHAT THAT LEAVES, SAID PLAINLY RATHER THAN LEFT AS A GAP: a `logs/` or
# `_to_delete/` directory created at depth later would still shadow a source
# path. THE RESIDUAL IS COVERED BY A CHECK AND NOT BY CARE — the checker beside
# the publication controls reads BOTH lists and refuses. Measured when it was
# written: no allowlisted path has a `logs` or `_to_delete` segment, and
# `src/app/lib/utils.ts` was the only entry any rule here shadowed.
#
# AND THE INSTRUMENT THAT SETTLES A GITIGNORE QUESTION IS NEITHER THIS COMMENT
# NOR THAT CHECKER: it is the publication preview, read beside what `git` itself
# answers for the same path. Two instruments that agree is the confirmation. A
# script reimplementing a fraction of git's matching is a second opinion.

# Handoff notes. Working documents about how a change was made, never part of
# the software, named here so they cannot reach a commit by being forgotten. The
# whole of `docs/` is left behind by the publication config, so this changes
# nothing about what ships; it is about this folder's own history.
docs/HANDOFF-*.md
